--- !u!114 &738763783
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 738763781}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: dfb91e39d5a15b443bb75e4ad9956136, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::InfoManager
  infoTitleText: {fileID: 1612118608}
  infoContentText: {fileID: 1395877383}
  previousInfoButton: {fileID: 2118830410}
  nextInfoButton: {fileID: 739600761}
  infoBackground: {fileID: 2065429924}
  chaptersJsonResourcePath: Localization/Film.en
  chapters:
  - title: Film
    content: "Born in Brussels in 1514 into a long line of court physicians, Andreas
      Vesalius grew up near the so-called \u201CHell Street,\u201D close to the Galgenberg,
      the hill where executions took place.\n\nAs a young boy, he observed the bodies
      of the condemned, an experience that awakened both his fascination and his
      scientific curiosity."
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: caf3044da6a939448ace1313f4cdf6c5, type: 3}
  - title: f1
    content: "Between 1533 and 1536, he studied in Paris under Jacques Dubois. Eager
      to deepen his anatomical knowledge, he secretly exhumed bodies from the Cemetery
      of the Innocents.\n\nInfluenced by the writings of al-Razi (Rhazes), his first
      publication in 1537 was a paraphrase of the ninth book of al-Razi\u2019s work.
      This marked a decisive shift away from Galenic dogma toward direct observation."
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c3474d51848ad4047b0703161e5391d8, type: 3}
  - title: f2
    content: 'Vesalius quickly gained fame in Venice and Padua. At just 23, he became
      a professor at the University of Padua. In 1543, he published De humani corporis
      fabrica, a groundbreaking anatomical treatise.


      He later served Emperor
      Charles V and married Anne Witekind in Brussels in 1544.'
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 48a79eafb99bec248a1ab19ca39673f9, type: 3}
  - title: f3
    content: 'Although protected by Emperor Philip II, his dissections provoked criticism
      from the Church and from traditional physicians.


      Accused before the
      Inquisition in Spain, he departed for Jerusalem in 1564. On his return journey,
      exhausted and ill, he died on the Greek island of Zante (Zakynthos) in October
      1564.'
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: d8f4e78290ec9c84faab619b21ced15e, type: 3}
